    <page>

    <exercise>
        <title>Exercise 3.19 (b)</title>
        
        
        <statement>
            <p>
                Prove that <m>\{12a + 3b: a, b\in\mathbb{Z}\} = \{3k: k\in\mathbb{Z}\}</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>

        <solution>
            <p>
                <em>Theorem. If <m>A= \{12a + 3b: a, b\in\mathbb{Z}\}</m> and <m>B= \{3k: k\in\mathbb{Z}\}</m> 
                    then <m>A=B</m> </em> </p>
                    <p>
                <em>Proof</em>. Suppose that <m>A= \{12a + 3b: a, b\in\mathbb{Z}\}</m> and <m>B= \{3k: k\in\mathbb{Z}\}</m>. 
                We will show <m>A=B</m> by showing that <m>A\subseteq B</m> and <m>B\subseteq A</m>. 
            </p>

            <p>
                First, suppose that <m>x\in A</m>. Then there exist integers <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> such that <m>x=12a+3b</m>.
                Thus,
                    <md>
                        x = 12 a + 3 b = 3 (4a+b).                    
                    </md>
                   Since the integers are closed under multiplicaton and addition, <m>4a+b\in\mathbb{Z}</m>. 
                   Therefore, <m>x=3k</m> for some integer <m>k=4a+b</m>, and so <m>x\in B</m>. Thus we have shown that <m>A\subseteq B</m>.
            </p>

            <p>
                Now we will show that <m>B\subseteq A</m>. 
                Suppose that <m>y\in B</m>. Then there exists an integer <m>k</m> such that <m>y=3k</m>.
                Let <m>a=0</m> and <m>b=k</m>. Then <m>a, b\in\mathbb{Z}</m> and
                    <md>
                        y = 3k = 12(0) + 3k = 12a + 3b.
                    </md>
                Thus <m>y\in A</m>, and we have shown that <m>B\subseteq A</m>.
            </p>

            <p>
                Since we have shown that <m>A\subseteq B</m> and <m>B\subseteq A</m>, it follows that <m>A=B</m>.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </exercise>

</page>
</worksheet>